@node Overview of Scheme
@nodedescription Overview of the Scheme language.
@chapter Overview of Scheme

@node Semantics
@section Semantics
This section gives an overview of Scheme's semantics.  A detailed
informal semantics is the subject of chapters 3 through 6.  For
reference purposes, section 7.2 (@ref{Formal semantics}) provides a
formal semantics of Scheme.

@cindex scope
@cindex static scope
@cindex lexical scope
Scheme is a statically scoped programming language.  Each use of a
variable is associated with a lexically apparent binding of that
variable.

@cindex types
@cindex dynamic types @seeentry{types}
Scheme is a dynamically typed language.  Types are associated with
values (also called objects) rather than with variables.  Statically
typed languages, by contrast, associate types with variables and
expressions as well as with values.

@cindex continuations @subentry extents of
@cindex extents
@cindex iteration
@cindex proper tail recursion @subentry implementation requirement
@cindex syntactic recursion
All objects created in the course of a Scheme computation, including
procedures and continuations, have unlimited extent.  No Scheme object
is ever destroyed.  The reason that implementations of Scheme do not
(usually!)@: run out of storage is that they are permitted to reclaim
the storage occupied by an object if they can prove that the object
cannot possibly matter to any future computation.  Implementations of
Scheme are required to be properly tail-recursive.  This allows the
execution of an iterative computation in constant space, even if the
iterative computation is described by a syntactically recursive
procedure.  Thus with a properly tail-recursive implementation,
iteration can be expressed using the ordinary procedure-call mechanics,
so that special iteration constructs are useful only as syntactic sugar.
@xref{Proper tail recursion}.

@cindex procedures @subentry as objects
@cindex continuations @subentry first-class status
Scheme procedures are objects in their own right.  Procedures can be
created dynamically, stored in data structures, returned as results of
procedures, and so on.  One distinguishing feature of Scheme is that
continuations, which in most other languages only operate behind the
scenes, also have @quotedbl{first-class} status.  Continuations are
useful for implementing a wide variety of advanced control constructs,
including non-local exits, backtracking, and coroutines.  @xref{Control
features}.

@cindex argument evaluation
@cindex argument passing
@cindex order of evaluation
@cindex pass by value
@cindex arithmetic @subentry exact @seeentry{exact arithmetic}
@cindex arithmetic @subentry inexact @seeentry{inexact arithmetic}
@cindex exact arithmetic
@cindex inexact arithmetic
Arguments to Scheme procedures are always passed by value, which means
that the actual argument expressions are evaluated before the procedure
gains control, regardless of whether the procedure needs the result of
the evaluation.  Scheme's model of arithmetic is designed to remain as
independent as possible of the particular ways in which numbers are
represented within a computer.  In Scheme, every integer is a rational
number, every rational is a real, and every real is a complex number.
Thus the distinction between integer and real arithmetic, so important
to many programming languages, does not appear in Scheme.  In its place
is a distinction between exact arithmetic, which corresponds to the
mathematical ideal, and inexact arithmetic on approximations.  Exact
arithmetic is not limited to integers.

@node Syntax
@section Syntax
@cindex prefix notation
@cindex Polish notation
@cindex programs as data
Scheme, like most dialects of Lisp, employs a fully parenthesized prefix
notation for programs and other data; the grammar of Scheme generates a
sublanguage of the language used for data.  An important consequence of
this simple, uniform representation is that Scheme programs and data can
easily be treated uniformly by other Scheme programs.  For example, the
@code{eval} procedure evaluates a Scheme program expressed as data.

@cindex lexical decomposition
@cindex syntactic decomposition
@cindex parse as data
@cindex parsing as data
The @code{read} procedure performs syntactic as well as lexical
decomposition of the data it reads.  The @code{read} procedure parses
its input as data (@pxref{External representations}), not as program.

The formal syntax of Scheme is described in section 7.1 (@ref{Formal
syntax}).

@node Notation and terminology
@section Notation and terminology
@node Base and optional features
@subsection Base and optional features
@cindex base library
@cindex libraries
Every identifier defined in this report appears in one or more of
several @nobr{@define{libraries}}.  Identifiers defined in the
@emph{base library} are not marked specially in the body of the report.
This library includes the core syntax of Scheme and generally useful
procedures that manipulate data.  For example, the variable @code{abs}
is bound to a procedure of one argument that computes the absolute value
of a number, and the variable @code{+} is bound to a procedure that
computes sums.  The full list of all the standard libraries and the
identifiers they export is given in Appendix A (@ref{Standard
Libraries}).

All implementations of Scheme:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Must provide the base library and all the identifiers exported from it.

@item
May provide or omit the other libraries given in this report, but each
library must either be provided in its entirety, exporting no additional
identifiers, or else omitted altogether.

@item
May provide other libraries not described in this report.

@item
May also extend the function of any identifier in this report, provided
the extensions are not in conflict with the language reported here.

@item
Must support portable code by providing a mode of operation in which the
lexical syntax does not conflict with the lexical syntax described in
this report.
@end itemize

@node Error situations and unspecified behavior
@subsection Error situations and unspecified behavior
@cindex error signaling
@cindex error situation
@cindex @quotedbl{an error is signaled} @sortas{an error is signaled}
@cindex signaling an error
@cindex non-continuable exception
When speaking of an error situation, this report uses the phrase
@quotedbl{an error is signaled} to indicate that implementations must
detect and report the error.  An error is signaled by raising a
non-continuable exception, as if by the procedure @code{raise} as
described in section 6.11 (@ref{Exceptions}).  The object raised is
implementation-dependent and need not be distinct from objects
previously used for the same purpose.  In addition to errors signaled in
situations described in this report, programmers can signal their own
errors and handle signaled errors.

@cindex @quotedbl{an error that satisfies @var{predicate}} @sortas{an error that satisfies}
The phrase @quotedbl{an error that satisfies @var{predicate} is
signaled} means that an error is signaled as above.  Furthermore, if the
object that is signaled is passed to the specified predicate (such as
@nc{file-error?} or @nc{read-error?}), the predicate returns @code{#t}.

@cindex @quotedbl{an error} @sortas{an error}
If such wording does not appear in the discussion of an error, then
implementations are not required to detect or report the error, though
they are encouraged to do so.  Such a situation is sometimes, but not
always, referred to with the phrase @quotedbl{an error}.  In such a
situation, an implementation may or may not signal an error; if it does
signal an error, the object that is signaled may or may not satisfy the
predicates @nc{error-object?}, @nc{file-error?}, or @nc{read-error?}.
Alternatively, implementations may provide non-portable extensions.

For example, it is an error for a procedure to be passed an argument of
a type that the procedure is not explicitly specified to handle, even
though such domain errors are seldom mentioned in this report.
Implementations may signal an error, extend a procedure’s domain of
definition to include such arguments, or fail catastrophically.

@cindex @quotedbl{may report a violation of an implementation restriction} @sortas{may report a violation of an implementation restriction}
@cindex implementation restrictions
This report uses the phrase @quotedbl{may report a violation of an
implementation restriction} to indicate circumstances under which an
implementation is permitted to report that it is unable to continue
execution of a correct program because of some restriction imposed by
the implementation.  Implemen@value{shy}ta@value{shy}tion restrictions
are discouraged, but implementations are encouraged to report violations
of implementation restrictions.

For example, an implementation may report a violation of an
implementation restriction if it does not have enough storage to run a
program, or if an arithmetic operation would produce an exact number
that is too large for the implementation to represent.

@cindex unspecified values
If the value of an expression is said to be @quotedbl{unspecified}, then
the expression must evaluate to some object without signaling an error,
but the value depends on the implementation; this report explicitly does
not say what value is returned.

@cindex RFC 2199
@cindex @quotedbl{must} @sortas{must} @seeentry{RFC 2199}
@cindex @quotedbl{must not} @sortas{must not} @seeentry{RFC 2199}
@cindex @quotedbl{shall} @sortas{shall} @seeentry{RFC 2199}
@cindex @quotedbl{shall not} @sortas{shall not} @seeentry{RFC 2199}
@cindex @quotedbl{should} @sortas{should} @seeentry{RFC 2199}
@cindex @quotedbl{should not} @sortas{should not} @seeentry{RFC 2199}
@cindex @quotedbl{may} @sortas{may} @seeentry{RFC 2199}
@cindex @quotedbl{required} @sortas{required} @seeentry{RFC 2199}
@cindex @quotedbl{recommended} @sortas{recommended} @seeentry{RFC 2199}
@cindex @quotedbl{optional} @sortas{optional} @seeentry{RFC 2199}
Finally, the words and phrases @quotedbl{must}, @quotedbl{must not},
@quotedbl{shall}, @quotedbl{shall not}, @quotedbl{should},
@quotedbl{should not}, @quotedbl{may}, @quotedbl{required},
@quotedbl{recommended}, and @quotedbl{optional}, although not
capitalized in this report, are to be interpreted as described in
@acronym{RFC 2119,Key words for use in @acronym{RFCs,Requests for
Comments} to Indicate Requirement Levels}@footnote{@value{refRFC2119}}.
They are used only with reference to implementer or implementation
behavior, not with reference to programmer or program behavior.

@node Entry format
@subsection Entry format
Chapters 4 (@ref{Expressions}) and 6 (@ref{Standard procedures}) are
organized into entries.  Each entry describes one language feature or a
group of related features, where a feature is either a syntactic
construct or a procedure.  An entry begins with one or more header lines
of the form

@defblock
@deftypeline @i{category} @/ @i{template}
@end defblock

for identifiers in the base library, or

@defblock
@deftypeline {@i{name} library @i{category}} @/ @i{template}
@end defblock

where @var{name} is the short name of a library as defined in Appendix A
(@ref{Standard Libraries}).

@cindex Syntactic variables
If @var{category} is @quotedbl{syntax}, the the entry describes an
expression type, and the template gives the syntax of the expression
type.  Components of expressions are designated by syntactic variables,
which are written using angle brackets, for example @angle{expression}
and @angle{variable}.  Syntactic variables are intended to denote
segments of program text; for example, @angle{expression} stands for any
string of characters which is a syntactically valid expression.  The
notation

@display
@angle{@parI{thing}} @value{defdots}
@end display

indicates zero or more occurrences of a @angle{thing}, and

@display
@angle{@parI{thing}} @angle{@parII{thing}} @value{defdots}
@end display

indicates one or more occurrences of a @angle{thing}.

@cindex auxiliary syntax
If @var{category} is @quotedbl{auxiliary syntax}, then the entry
describes a syntax binding that occurs only as part of specific
surrounding expressions.  Any use as an independent syntactic construct
or variable is an error.

If @var{category} is @quotedbl{procedure}, then the entry describes a
procedure, and the header line gives a template for a call to the
procedure@inlinefmtifelse{info,.,. Argument names in the template are
@i{italicized}.}  Thus the header line

@defblock
@defline procedure vector-ref vector k
@end defblock

indicates that the procedure bound to the @nc{vector-ref} variable takes
two arguments, a vector @var{vector} and an exact non-negative integer
@var{k} (see below).

The header lines

@defblock
@defline procedure make-vector k
@defline procedure make-vector k fill
@end defblock

indicate that the @nc{make-vector} procedure must be defined to take
either one or two arguments.

It is an error for a procedure to be presented with an argument that it
is not specified to handle.  For succinctness, we follow the convention
that if an argument name is also the name of a type listed in section
3.2 (@ref{Disjointness of types}), then it is an error if that argument
is not of the named type.  For example, the header line for
@nc{vector-ref} given above dictates that the first argument to
@nc{vector-ref} is a vector.  The following naming conventions also
imply type restrictions:

@cindex parameter naming conventions
@indentedblock
@multitable @columnfractions .34 .66
@item @i{alist} @tab association list (list of pairs)
@item @i{boolean} @tab boolean value (@code{#t} or @code{#f})
@item @i{byte} @tab exact integer 0 @leq{} @var{byte} < 256
@item @i{bytevector} @tab bytevector
@item @i{char} @tab character
@item @i{end} @tab exact non-negative integer
@item @i{k, @parI{k}, @value{defdots} @parj{k}, @value{defdots}} @tab exact non-negative integer
@item @i{letter} @tab alphabetic character
@item @i{list, @parI{list}, @value{defdots} @parj{list}, @value{defdots}} @tab list (@pxref{Pairs and lists})
@item @i{n, @parI{n}, @value{defdots} @parj{n}, @value{defdots}} @tab integer
@item @i{obj} @tab any object
@item @i{pair} @tab pair
@item @i{proc} @tab procedure
@item @i{q, @parI{q}, @value{defdots} @parj{q}, @value{defdots}} @tab rational number
@item @i{start} @tab exact non-negative integer
@item @i{string} @tab string
@item @i{symbol} @tab symbol
@item @i{thunk} @tab zero-argument procedure
@item @i{vector} @tab vector
@item @i{x, @parI{x}, @value{defdots} @parj{x}, @value{defdots}} @tab real number
@item @i{y, @parI{y}, @value{defdots} @parj{y}, @value{defdots}} @tab real number
@item @i{z, @parI{z}, @value{defdots} @parj{z}, @value{defdots}} @tab complex number
@end multitable
@end indentedblock

The names @define{start} and @define{end} are used as indexes into
strings, vectors, and bytevectors.  Their use implies the following:

@itemize @bullet
@item
It is an error if @var{start} is greater than @var{end}.

@item
It is an error if @var{end} is greater than the length of the string,
vector, or bytevector.

@item
If @var{start} is omitted, it is assumed to be zero.

@item
If @var{end} is omitted, it assumed to be the length of the string,
vector, or bytevector.

@item
The index @var{start} is always inclusive and the index @var{end} is
always exclusive.  As an example, consider a string.  If @var{start} and
@var{end} are the same, an empty substring is referred to, and if
@var{start} is zero and @var{end} is the length of @var{string}, then
the entire string is referred to.
@end itemize

@node Evaluation examples
@subsection Evaluation examples
The symbol @quotedbl{@t{@result{}}} in program examples is read
@quotedbl{evaluates to}.  For example,

@lisp
(* 5 8) @ok{40}
@end lisp

means that the expression @code{(* 5 8)} evaluates to the object
@code{40}.  Or, more precisely: the expression given by the sequence of
characters @samp{(* 5 8)} evaluates, in an environment containing the
base library, to an object that can be represented externally by the
sequence of characters @samp{40}.  @xref{Evaluation examples} for a
discussion of external representations of objects.

@ifinfo
The symbol @quotedbl{@t{@print{}}} in program examples is read
@quotedbl{prints}.  For example,

@lisp
(display (* 5 8)) @output{40}
                  @unspecified{unspecified}
@end lisp

means that the expression @code{(@nobr{display} (* 5 8))} prints the
sequence of characters @samp{40} to the current output port and
evaluates to an unspecified value.

@end ifinfo
@node Naming conventions
@subsection Naming conventions
@cindex ?
@cindex predicates @subentry naming conventions
By convention, @samp{?} is the final character of the names of
procedures that always return a boolean value.  Such procedures are
called @define{predicates}.  Predicates are generally understood to be
side-effect free, except that they may raise an exception when passed
the wrong type of argument.

@cindex !
@cindex mutation procedures @subentry naming conventions
Similarly, @samp{!} is the final character of the names of procedures
that store values into previously allocated locations (@pxref{Storage
model}).  Such procedures are called @define{mutation procedures}.  The
value returned by a mutation procedure is unspecified.

@cindex ->
@cindex coercion procedures
@cindex coercion procedures @subentry naming conventions
By convention, @samp{->} appears within the names of procedures that
take an object of one type and return an analogous object of another
type.  For example, @nc{list->vector} takes a list and returns a vector
whose elements are the same as those of the list.

@cindex command
A @define{command} is a procedure that does not return useful values to
its continuation.

@cindex thunk
A @define{thunk} is a procedure that does not accept arguments.
